-- title:
--   Nonvanishing of dx for a separating transcendental element
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, $F$ a field, and let $F$ be a $K$-algebra. Let $x \in F$ be transcendental over $K$, i.e. `Transcendental K x`: $x$ is not a root of any nonzero polynomial over $K$. Assume further that $F$ is a separable algebra over the intermediate field $K(x) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K {x}` generated by $x$, that is, every element of $F$ is algebraic with separable minimal polynomial over $K(x)$. The conclusion is that the universal derivation applied to $x$, namely `KaehlerDifferential.D K F x` $= dx$, is nonzero in the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega_{F/K}$ of $F$ over $K$. This is the nonvanishing half only: the statement asserts $dx \neq 0$, not that $dx$ is an $F$-basis of $\Omega_{F/K}$, although the proof establishes the stronger fact along the way.
--
--   This is the classical nonvanishing of the differential of a separating element of a function field, underlying the fact that $\Omega_{F/K}$ is one-dimensional over $F$ with basis $dx$. It is used throughout the treatment of differentials on curves, for instance in defining and computing the order of a differential at a place and in the study of torsion in the degree-zero part of the Picard group.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_KaehlerDifferential_D_ne_zero_of_transcendental.lean

import Mathlib.RingTheory.Kaehler.Basic
import Mathlib.FieldTheory.IntermediateField.Adjoin.Basic
import Mathlib.FieldTheory.Separable
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Algebraic.Defs

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency.types false

theorem KaehlerDifferential.D_ne_zero_of_transcendental (K : Type*) [Field K] {F : Type*} [Field F] [Algebra K F] (x : F) (hx : Transcendental K x)
    [Algebra.IsSeparable (IntermediateField.adjoin K ({x} : Set F)) F] :
    KaehlerDifferential.D K F x ≠ 0 := by sorry
